IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ssc-core`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (United Kingdom), 1992 c. 4.
-- LAYER 1, part ssc6: Part XII, Statutory Maternity Pay (ss 164-171).
-- Lines 3241-3384 of ukpga-1992-4.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a converter script.
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here is "verified against the official text".
--
-- How to read a result (the rule of 2026-10-29, assumed and not ruled by a lawyer):
--   JUST FALSE answers "the text says no in its own words" and carries a comment
--       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "<the quoted words>"
--   NOTHING answers "not covered" and carries a comment  -- NOTHING-kind (<why>)
--   A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Money is a NUMBER in pounds sterling; a figure is weekly unless its name says otherwise.
-- Dates are written `Date day month year`.
-- Every top-level name begins with the label of the provision it implements.
-- Numbers that regulations supply ("prescribed") are inputs. Numbers the Act states in its
-- own words (26 weeks, 28 days, 52 weeks, 90 per cent, 92 per cent) are constants below, so that
-- an order or regulation that substituted another number would change the answers.

§ `Part XII -- Statutory Maternity Pay`

§§ `s 164 Entitlement and liability to pay`

-- Helper (it implements no provision): the three-valued AND of a list of answers.
-- JUST FALSE if any answer is JUST FALSE; JUST TRUE if every answer is JUST TRUE; NOTHING otherwise.
-- The JUST FALSE it returns is the "no" of the calling rule, whose own comment quotes the text.
GIVEN answers IS A LIST OF MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(1) all of these answers hold` MEANS
    CONSIDER answers
    WHEN EMPTY THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN first FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        CONSIDER first
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN `s 164(1) all of these answers hold` rest
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            CONSIDER `s 164(1) all of these answers hold` rest
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- The number of weeks of continuous employment that subsection (2)(a) asks for.
-- src:3245 | (a) that she has been in employed earner’s employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks ending with the week immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement but has ceased to work for him ... ;
@ref s 164(2)(a) (src:3245)
`s 164(2)(a) the number of weeks of continuous employment required` MEANS 26

-- Plain gloss: she has worked for the employer for at least 26 weeks running up to the "relevant
-- week" (the 15th week before the week the baby is due), in a job that counts as employed
-- earner's employment, and has since stopped working for that employer.
-- The converter shows "..." after "him" (src:3245): words repealed from the paragraph. The
-- surviving words are encoded.
-- src:3244 | (2) The conditions mentioned in subsection (1) above are—
-- src:3245 | (a) that she has been in employed earner’s employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks ending with the week immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement but has ceased to work for him ... ;
@ref s 164(2)(a) (src:3244-3245)
GIVEN service IS A `Service for statutory pay`
      `her employment with the employer is employed earner's employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `she has ceased to work for him` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(2)(a) she has been in employed earner's employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks and has ceased to work for him` MEANS
    IF     `her employment with the employer is employed earner's employment`
       AND service's `weeks of continuous employment with the employer ending with the relevant week`
               AT LEAST `s 164(2)(a) the number of weeks of continuous employment required`
       AND `she has ceased to work for him`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions mentioned in subsection (1) above are—"

-- Plain gloss: over the 8 weeks up to the relevant week she earned, on average, at least the
-- National Insurance lower earnings limit that applied (the limit is set by regulations, so the
-- caller supplies it as the figure in force at the time).
-- src:3246 | (b) that her normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks ending with the week immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement are not less than the lower earnings limit in force under section 5(1)(a) above immediately before the commencement of the 14th week before the expected week of confinement; and
@ref s 164(2)(b) (src:3246)
GIVEN service IS A `Service for statutory pay`
      `s 5(1)(a) the lower earnings limit in force at the time in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(2)(b) her normal weekly earnings are not less than the lower earnings limit` MEANS
    IF service's `normal weekly earnings in pounds over the period the section names`
           AT LEAST `s 5(1)(a) the lower earnings limit in force at the time in pounds`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions mentioned in subsection (1) above are—"

-- Plain gloss: she is pregnant, and has either got as far as the 11th week before the week the
-- baby is due or has already been confined before that.
-- src:3247 | (c) that she has become pregnant and has reached, or been confined before reaching, the commencement of the 11th week before the expected week of confinement.
@ref s 164(2)(c) (src:3247)
GIVEN `she has become pregnant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `she has reached the commencement of the 11th week before the expected week of confinement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `she was confined before reaching that commencement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(2)(c) she has become pregnant and has reached, or been confined before reaching, the commencement of the 11th week` MEANS
    IF     `she has become pregnant`
       AND (   `she has reached the commencement of the 11th week before the expected week of confinement`
            OR `she was confined before reaching that commencement`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions mentioned in subsection (1) above are—"

-- The three conditions together.
-- src:3244 | (2) The conditions mentioned in subsection (1) above are—
@ref s 164(2) (src:3244)
GIVEN `condition (a) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (b) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `condition (c) is satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(2) the conditions mentioned in subsection (1) are satisfied` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The conditions mentioned in subsection (1) above are—"
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): a limb that depends on a figure or fact regulations supply is NOTHING when the caller does not supply it
    `s 164(1) all of these answers hold` (LIST `condition (a) is satisfied`, `condition (b) is satisfied`, `condition (c) is satisfied`)

-- Plain gloss: the notice a woman must give. She must tell the person who will pay when she
-- expects the payments to start, at least 28 days before that date, or, if that was not
-- reasonably practicable, as soon as was reasonably practicable. "Only if" makes this a condition
-- of entitlement: without it, no payments.
-- src:3249 | (4) A woman shall be entitled to payments of statutory maternity pay only if—
-- src:3250 | (a) she gives the person who will be liable to pay it notice of the date from which she expects his liability to pay her statutory maternity pay to begin; and
-- src:3251 | (b) the notice is given at least 28 days before that date or, if that is not reasonably practicable, as soon as is reasonably practicable.
@ref s 164(4) (src:3249-3251)
`s 164(4)(b) the number of days of notice required` MEANS 28

@ref s 164(4) (src:3249-3251)
GIVEN `she gave the person who will be liable to pay it notice of the date from which she expects his liability to pay her statutory maternity pay to begin` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the number of days before that date on which the notice was given` IS A NUMBER
      `it was not reasonably practicable to give the notice at least 28 days before that date` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the notice was given as soon as was reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(4) the notice condition for payments of statutory maternity pay is met` MEANS
    IF     `she gave the person who will be liable to pay it notice of the date from which she expects his liability to pay her statutory maternity pay to begin`
       AND (   `the number of days before that date on which the notice was given`
                   AT LEAST `s 164(4)(b) the number of days of notice required`
            OR (`it was not reasonably practicable to give the notice at least 28 days before that date`
                AND `the notice was given as soon as was reasonably practicable`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A woman shall be entitled to payments of statutory maternity pay only if"

-- Plain gloss: the woman is entitled to the payments if she is, or has been, an employee and
-- meets the conditions of subsection (2) and the notice condition of subsection (4). The
-- entitlement is "in accordance with the following provisions of this Part", so the weeks, the
-- rate and the bars in ss 165 and 166 still apply.
-- src:3243 | (1) Where a woman who is or has been an employee satisfies the conditions set out in this section, she shall be entitled, in accordance with the following provisions of this Part of this Act, to payments to be known as “statutory maternity pay”.
@ref s 164(1) (src:3243)
GIVEN `s 171(1) she is an employee` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 164(2) the conditions mentioned in subsection (1) are satisfied` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 164(4) the notice condition for payments of statutory maternity pay is met` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(1) the woman is entitled to payments of statutory maternity pay` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a woman who is or has been an employee satisfies the conditions set out in this section" (marginal reading (a): a failed "where"/"if" counts as the text's no)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): a prescribed modification under subsection (9)(e) or (ea), or a figure regulations supply, is not applied here
    `s 164(1) all of these answers hold` (LIST `s 171(1) she is an employee`, `s 164(2) the conditions mentioned in subsection (1) are satisfied`, `s 164(4) the notice condition for payments of statutory maternity pay is met`)

-- Plain gloss: the person who must pay is any person she has been an employee of, in the way
-- subsection (2)(a) describes. The Act names nobody else here; regulations can put the liability
-- on the Commissioners (subsection (9)(b)).
-- src:3248 | (3) The liability to make payments of statutory maternity pay to a woman is a liability of any person of whom she has been an employee as mentioned in subsection (2)(a) above.
@ref s 164(3) (src:3248)
GIVEN `she has been an employee of the person as mentioned in subsection (2)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(3) the liability to make payments of statutory maternity pay is the person's` MEANS
    IF `she has been an employee of the person as mentioned in subsection (2)(a)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection names who is liable, and says nothing of anyone else; subsection (9)(b) can make the Commissioners liable

-- Plain gloss: the notice must be put in writing if the person who has to pay asks for that.
-- src:3252 | (5) The notice shall be in writing if the person who is liable to pay the woman statutory maternity pay so requests.
@ref s 164(5) (src:3252)
GIVEN `the person who is liable to pay the woman statutory maternity pay requests it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(5) the notice shall be in writing` MEANS
    IF `the person who is liable to pay the woman statutory maternity pay requests it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The notice shall be in writing if the person who is liable to pay the woman statutory maternity pay so requests." (marginal reading (a): a failed "if" counts as the text's no)

-- Plain gloss: a contract term cannot take away or cut down her statutory maternity pay, or make
-- an employee pay towards the employer's costs of it. The word "extent" limits the voidness to
-- what the agreement purports to do.
-- src:3253 | (6) Any agreement shall be void to the extent that it purports—
-- src:3254 | (a) to exclude, limit or otherwise modify any provision of this Part of this Act; or
-- src:3255 | (b) to require an employee or former employee to contribute (whether directly or indirectly) towards any costs incurred by her employer or former employer under this Part of this Act.
@ref s 164(6) (src:3253-3255)
GIVEN `the agreement purports to exclude, limit or otherwise modify a provision of Part XII` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the agreement purports to require an employee or former employee to contribute, directly or indirectly, towards costs incurred by her employer or former employer under Part XII` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(6) the agreement is void to the extent that it purports` MEANS
    IF     `the agreement purports to exclude, limit or otherwise modify a provision of Part XII`
        OR `the agreement purports to require an employee or former employee to contribute, directly or indirectly, towards costs incurred by her employer or former employer under Part XII`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to the extent that it purports"

-- Plain gloss: the safe harbour. An agreement that lets the employer make deductions from her
-- statutory maternity pay is not void under (6)(a) if the employer could make the same
-- deductions from her contractual pay for the same period, or could if it were paying her.
-- src:3256 | (7) For the avoidance of doubt, any agreement between an employer and an employee authorising any deductions from statutory maternity pay which the employer is liable to pay to the employee in respect of any period shall not be void by virtue of subsection (6)(a) above if the employer—
-- src:3257 | (a) is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from any contractual remuneration which he is liable to pay in respect of the same period, or
-- src:3258 | (b) would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period.
@ref s 164(7) (src:3256-3258)
GIVEN `the agreement authorises deductions from statutory maternity pay that the employer is liable to pay in respect of a period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from contractual remuneration he is liable to pay in respect of the same period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employer would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 164(7) the agreement is protected from being void by virtue of subsection (6)(a)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the agreement authorises deductions from statutory maternity pay that the employer is liable to pay in respect of a period`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks only of "any agreement ... authorising any deductions"
        IF    `the employer is authorised by that or another agreement to make the same deductions from contractual remuneration he is liable to pay in respect of the same period`
           OR `the employer would be so authorised if he were liable to pay contractual remuneration in respect of that period`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall not be void by virtue of subsection (6)(a) above if the employer" (marginal reading (a): a failed "if" counts as the text's no)

§§ `s 165 The maternity pay period`

-- Plain gloss: the longest the maternity pay period can be.
-- src:3279 | (1) Statutory maternity pay shall be payable, subject to the provisions of this Part of this Act, in respect of each week during a prescribed period (“the maternity pay period”) of a duration not exceeding 52 weeks.
@ref s 165(1) (src:3279)
`s 165(1) the longest maternity pay period in weeks` MEANS 52

-- Plain gloss: "week" in ss 165(1), (4) and (6) is a week that starts on the day of the week the
-- maternity pay period starts (subsection (8)); in s 166(1) it is any 7 days (s 166(1A)); in the rest
-- of Part XII it is a Sunday-to-Saturday week unless regulations prescribe another (s 171(1A)).
-- src:3300 | (8) In subsections (1), (4) and (6) “week” means a period of seven days beginning with the day of the week on which the maternity pay period begins.
@ref s 165(8) (src:3300)
`s 165(8) the week convention of subsections (1), (4) and (6)` MEANS `a period of 7 days beginning with the day on which a stated period begins`

-- src:3305 | (1A) In subsection (1) “week” means any period of seven days.
@ref s 166(1A) (src:3305)
`s 166(1A) the week convention of section 166(1)` MEANS `any period of 7 days`

-- src:3369 | (1A) In this Part, except section 165(1), (4) and (6), section 166(1) and paragraph 3(2) of Schedule 13, “week” means a period of 7 days beginning with Sunday or such other period as may be prescribed in relation to any particular case or class of case.
@ref s 171(1A) (src:3369)
`s 171(1A) the week convention of Part XII` MEANS `a period of 7 days beginning with Sunday or another prescribed period`

-- Plain gloss: the Sunday that starts the week a given day falls in. The day count `Day d` is a
-- multiple of 7 on a Sunday (the tests pin this on Sunday 4 January 2026).
-- src:3369 | (1A) In this Part, except section 165(1), (4) and (6), section 166(1) and paragraph 3(2) of Schedule 13, “week” means a period of 7 days beginning with Sunday or such other period as may be prescribed in relation to any particular case or class of case.
@ref s 171(1A) (src:3369)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 171(1A) the first day of the week containing the day` MEANS
    IF `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case`
    THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "or such other period as may be prescribed"
    ELSE JUST (day MINUS ((Day day) MODULO 7))

-- Plain gloss: the week the conditions of s 164(2)(a) and (b) are tested against is "the week
-- immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement": the 15th week
-- before the week the baby is due, a Sunday-to-Saturday week unless regulations prescribe
-- another (s 171(1A)). The caller names any day in the expected week of confinement. The text does
-- not call it "the relevant week"; ssc7's s 171ZZ16 asks for it by that name (seam S30).
-- src:3245 | (a) that she has been in employed earner’s employment with an employer for a continuous period of at least 26 weeks ending with the week immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement but has ceased to work for him ... ;
-- src:3246 | (b) that her normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks ending with the week immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement are not less than the lower earnings limit in force under section 5(1)(a) above immediately before the commencement of the 14th week before the expected week of confinement; and
@ref s 164(2)(a), (b) (src:3245-3246)
GIVEN `a day in the expected week of confinement` IS A DATE
      `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 164 the relevant week` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 171(1A) the first day of the week containing the day` `a day in the expected week of confinement` `regulations prescribe another period in place of a week beginning with Sunday for the case`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the week is the one "as may be prescribed"
    WHEN JUST expectedWeek THEN
        JUST (Period WITH
                  `the first day` IS expectedWeek MINUS (15 TIMES 7)
                  `the last day` IS expectedWeek MINUS ((14 TIMES 7) PLUS 1))

-- Plain gloss: the day the maternity pay period begins. Unless subsection (3) or (7) says
-- otherwise, it is the first day of the 11th week before the week the baby is due, counting the
-- week before the due week as the 1st week before it.
-- src:3280 | (2) Subject to subsections (3) and (7), the maternity pay period shall begin with the 11th week before the expected week of confinement.
@ref s 165(2) (src:3280)
GIVEN `the first day of the expected week of confinement` IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 165(2) the day on which the maternity pay period begins` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the first day of the expected week of confinement`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the expected week of confinement is a fact the caller supplies
    WHEN JUST firstDay THEN JUST (firstDay MINUS (11 TIMES 7))

-- Plain gloss: where regulations set a later start, it cannot be later than the day after she is
-- confined. Before the first day of the 11th week there is nothing in this subsection to say.
-- src:3281 | (3) Cases may be prescribed in which the first day of the period is to be a prescribed day after the beginning of the 11th week before the expected week of confinement, but not later than the day immediately following the day on which she is confined.
@ref s 165(3) (src:3281)
GIVEN `the prescribed first day of the maternity pay period` IS A DATE
      `the day on which the 11th week before the expected week of confinement begins` IS A DATE
      `the day on which she is confined` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 165(3) the prescribed first day is permitted` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the prescribed first day of the maternity pay period` GREATER THAN (`the day on which she is confined` PLUS 1)
            THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "but not later than the day immediately following the day on which she is confined"
        IF `the prescribed first day of the maternity pay period` GREATER THAN `the day on which the 11th week before the expected week of confinement begins`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a prescribed day after the beginning of the 11th week"

-- Plain gloss: the maternity pay period is a run of whole weeks, at most 52, starting on the
-- day given. It is a duration regulations prescribe, so the caller supplies it.
-- src:3279 | (1) Statutory maternity pay shall be payable, subject to the provisions of this Part of this Act, in respect of each week during a prescribed period (“the maternity pay period”) of a duration not exceeding 52 weeks.
@ref s 165(1) (src:3279)
GIVEN `the first day of the maternity pay period` IS A DATE
      `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 165(1) the maternity pay period` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks` GREATER THAN `s 165(1) the longest maternity pay period in weeks`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "of a duration not exceeding 52 weeks"
        IF `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks` AT MOST 0
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): no period of a duration of no weeks
        OTHERWISE JUST (Period WITH
                            `the first day` IS `the first day of the maternity pay period`
                            `the last day` IS (`the first day of the maternity pay period` PLUS ((`the duration of the prescribed period in weeks` TIMES 7) MINUS 1)))

-- The same period, under the name ssc2's s 35 asks for it by (seam S6): the period does not
-- depend on whether she is in fact entitled, so it is the same function.
@ref s 165(1) (src:3279)
GIVEN `the first day of the maternity pay period` IS A DATE
      `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 165(1) the maternity pay period if she were entitled to statutory maternity pay` MEANS
    `s 165(1) the maternity pay period` `the first day of the maternity pay period` `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks`

-- Plain gloss: regulations can shorten the maternity pay period for a woman. The number of weeks
-- it is shortened to is prescribed (seam S27: ssc7 asks for it by this name). A shortened period
-- is shorter than the period it replaces.
-- src:3282 | (3A) Regulations may provide for the duration of the maternity pay period as it applies to a woman to be reduced, subject to prescribed restrictions and conditions.
@ref s 165(3A) (src:3282)
GIVEN `the first day of the maternity pay period` IS A DATE
      `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks` IS A NUMBER
      `s 165(3A) the number of weeks to which the maternity pay period is reduced` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 165(3A) the maternity pay period as reduced` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 165(3A) the number of weeks to which the maternity pay period is reduced` AT MOST 0
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection does not say what a reduction to no weeks is
        IF `s 165(3A) the number of weeks to which the maternity pay period is reduced` LESS THAN `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks`
            THEN `s 165(1) the maternity pay period` `the first day of the maternity pay period` `s 165(3A) the number of weeks to which the maternity pay period is reduced`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to be reduced"

-- Plain gloss: the answer ssc2's s 35(3E) asks for (seam S6). A week for which she would have
-- been paid but for the shortening is a week that falls in the unreduced period and after the end
-- of the reduced one, in a case where she would otherwise be entitled.
-- src:3282 | (3A) Regulations may provide for the duration of the maternity pay period as it applies to a woman to be reduced, subject to prescribed restrictions and conditions.
@ref s 165(3A) (src:3282)
GIVEN `she would be entitled to statutory maternity pay apart from the reduction` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the first day of the week` IS A DATE
      `the maternity pay period before the reduction` IS A Period
      `the maternity pay period as reduced` IS A Period
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 165(3A) the woman would be entitled to statutory maternity pay for the week but for the reduction in the maternity pay period` MEANS
    IF     `the first day of the week` AT LEAST `the maternity pay period before the reduction`'s `the first day`
       AND `the first day of the week` AT MOST `the maternity pay period before the reduction`'s `the last day`
       AND `the first day of the week` GREATER THAN `the maternity pay period as reduced`'s `the last day`
    THEN `she would be entitled to statutory maternity pay apart from the reduction`        -- JUST FALSE here is the answer of s 164, whose own comment quotes the text
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "in respect of each week during a prescribed period"; a week inside the reduced period, or outside the unreduced one, is not a week lost to the reduction

-- Plain gloss: if regulations shorten the period, the shortened period must end (a) after a
-- prescribed number of days from the day she is confined and (b) while at least a prescribed
-- part of the original period is still to run. Both limbs are numbers regulations prescribe.
-- src:3283 | (3B) Regulations under subsection (3A) are to secure that the reduced period ends at a time—
-- src:3284 | (a) after a prescribed period beginning with the day on which the woman is confined, and
-- src:3285 | (b) when at least a prescribed part of the maternity pay period remains unexpired.
@ref s 165(3B) (src:3283-3285)
GIVEN `the day on which the reduced period ends` IS A DATE
      `the day on which she is confined` IS A DATE
      `the prescribed period beginning with the day on which she is confined, in days` IS A NUMBER
      `the last day of the maternity pay period before the reduction` IS A DATE
      `the prescribed part of the maternity pay period that must remain unexpired, in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 165(3B) the reduced period ends at a time the regulations may allow` MEANS
    IF     `the day on which the reduced period ends`
               GREATER THAN (`the day on which she is confined` PLUS (`the prescribed period beginning with the day on which she is confined, in days` MINUS 1))
       AND (`the last day of the maternity pay period before the reduction` MINUS `the day on which the reduced period ends`)
               AT LEAST `the prescribed part of the maternity pay period that must remain unexpired, in days`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection says what the regulations "are to secure" and nothing of what follows if they do not

-- Plain gloss: unless regulations say otherwise, the employer who is paying does not have to pay
-- statutory maternity pay for a week in which she does any work under a contract of service with
-- him ("shall not be payable ... except in such cases as may be prescribed").
-- src:3292 | (4) Except in such cases as may be prescribed, statutory maternity pay shall not be payable to a woman by a person in respect of any week during any part of which she works under a contract of service with him.
@ref s 165(4) (src:3292)
GIVEN `she works under a contract of service with the person during any part of the week` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the case is one prescribed under subsection (4)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 165(4) statutory maternity pay is payable by the person for the week` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `she works under a contract of service with the person during any part of the week` AND `the case is one prescribed under subsection (4)`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Except in such cases as may be prescribed"
        IF `she works under a contract of service with the person during any part of the week`
            THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "statutory maternity pay shall not be payable to a woman by a person in respect of any week during any part of which she works under a contract of service with him"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection bars payment for a week of work and does not itself say the other weeks are payable

-- Plain gloss: the bar in (4) bites on work under a contract of service whether the contract
-- already existed before the maternity pay period or was made later. Whether it existed before
-- makes no difference, so the second input is deliberately unused.
-- src:3293 | (5) It is immaterial for the purposes of subsection (4) above whether the work referred to in that subsection is work under a contract of service which existed immediately before the maternity pay period or a contract of service which did not so exist.
@ref s 165(5) (src:3293)
GIVEN `the work is work under a contract of service with the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract of service existed immediately before the maternity pay period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 165(5) the work counts for subsection (4) whichever contract it is under` MEANS
    IF `the work is work under a contract of service with the person`
    THEN JUST TRUE        -- the second input is immaterial: "It is immaterial ... whether the work ... is work under a contract of service which existed immediately before the maternity pay period or ... which did not so exist"
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection speaks only of work under a contract of service

-- Plain gloss: after she has had the baby, she is not paid for a week in which she does any work
-- for an employer who is not liable to pay her statutory maternity pay, unless regulations say otherwise.
-- src:3294 | (6) Except in such cases as may be prescribed, statutory maternity pay shall not be payable to a woman in respect of any week after she has been confined and during any part of which she works for any employer who is not liable to pay her statutory maternity pay.
@ref s 165(6) (src:3294)
GIVEN `the week is after she has been confined` IS A BOOLEAN
      `during part of the week she works for an employer who is not liable to pay her statutory maternity pay` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the case is one prescribed under subsection (6)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 165(6) statutory maternity pay is payable for the week after confinement` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the week is after she has been confined` AND `during part of the week she works for an employer who is not liable to pay her statutory maternity pay` AND `the case is one prescribed under subsection (6)`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Except in such cases as may be prescribed"
        IF `the week is after she has been confined` AND `during part of the week she works for an employer who is not liable to pay her statutory maternity pay`
            THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "statutory maternity pay shall not be payable to a woman in respect of any week after she has been confined and during any part of which she works for any employer who is not liable to pay her statutory maternity pay"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection bars payment for such a week and does not itself say the other weeks are payable

-- Plain gloss: the first day of the 7-day week, counted from the day the maternity pay period
-- begins, that a given day falls in (the "week" of subsections (1), (4) and (6)).
-- src:3300 | (8) In subsections (1), (4) and (6) “week” means a period of seven days beginning with the day of the week on which the maternity pay period begins.
@ref s 165(8) (src:3300)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      `the first day of the maternity pay period` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 165(8) the first day of the week of the maternity pay period containing the day` MEANS
    IF day LESS THAN `the first day of the maternity pay period`
    THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the weeks are counted from the day the maternity pay period begins
    ELSE JUST (day MINUS ((day MINUS `the first day of the maternity pay period`) MODULO 7))

§§ `s 166 Rate of statutory maternity pay`

-- The percentage of normal weekly earnings that makes up the earnings-related rate.
-- src:3306 | (2) The earnings-related rate is a weekly rate equivalent to 90 per cent of a woman’s normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement.
@ref s 166(2) (src:3306)
`s 166(2) the percentage of normal weekly earnings` MEANS 90%

-- Plain gloss: 90 per cent of her normal weekly earnings over the 8 weeks up to the 14th week
-- before the week the baby is due.
@ref s 166(2) (src:3306)
GIVEN `her normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 166(2) the earnings-related rate in pounds` MEANS
    `her normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement, in pounds`
        TIMES `s 166(2) the percentage of normal weekly earnings`

-- Plain gloss: the number of weeks at the earnings-related rate.
-- src:3303 | (a) at the earnings-related rate, in respect of the first 6 weeks in respect of which it is payable; and
@ref s 166(1)(a) (src:3303)
`s 166(1)(a) the number of weeks payable at the earnings-related rate` MEANS 6

-- Plain gloss: for the first 6 weeks in which statutory maternity pay is payable she gets the
-- earnings-related rate; for the rest of the maternity pay period, the lower of that rate and the
-- weekly rate regulations prescribe. The week number counts the first week she is paid as week 1.
-- src:3302 | (1) Statutory maternity pay shall be payable to a woman—
-- src:3303 | (a) at the earnings-related rate, in respect of the first 6 weeks in respect of which it is payable; and
-- src:3304 | (b) at whichever is the lower of the earnings-related rate and such weekly rate as may be prescribed, in respect of the remaining portion of the maternity pay period.
@ref s 166(1) (src:3302-3304)
GIVEN `the number of the week for which statutory maternity pay is payable, counting the first such week as week 1` IS A NUMBER
      `her normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `s 166(1)(b) the weekly rate prescribed in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 166(1) the weekly rate of statutory maternity pay for the week in pounds` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the number of the week for which statutory maternity pay is payable, counting the first such week as week 1` LESS THAN 1
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): there is no week before the first week in which it is payable
        IF `the number of the week for which statutory maternity pay is payable, counting the first such week as week 1` GREATER THAN `s 165(1) the longest maternity pay period in weeks`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the rate is for weeks of "the maternity pay period", which does not exceed 52 weeks
        IF `the number of the week for which statutory maternity pay is payable, counting the first such week as week 1` AT MOST `s 166(1)(a) the number of weeks payable at the earnings-related rate`
            THEN JUST (`s 166(2) the earnings-related rate in pounds` `her normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement, in pounds`)
        OTHERWISE JUST (min (`s 166(2) the earnings-related rate in pounds` `her normal weekly earnings for the period of 8 weeks immediately preceding the 14th week before the expected week of confinement, in pounds`) `s 166(1)(b) the weekly rate prescribed in pounds`)

-- Plain gloss: the weekly rate regulations prescribe for the later weeks must not be below the
-- weekly rate of statutory sick pay (s 157(1): the figure the Act states, £123.25; the caller
-- supplies it, because s 157 is in Part XI).
-- src:3307 | (3) The weekly rate prescribed under subsection (1)(b) above must not be less than the weekly rate of statutory sick pay for the time being specified in section 157(1) above or, if two or more such rates are for the time being so specified, the higher or highest of those rates.
@ref s 166(3) (src:3307)
GIVEN `s 166(1)(b) the weekly rate prescribed in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `s 157(1) the weekly rate of statutory sick pay specified in section 157(1) in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 166(3) the weekly rate prescribed is not less than the weekly rate of statutory sick pay` MEANS
    IF `s 166(1)(b) the weekly rate prescribed in pounds` AT LEAST `s 157(1) the weekly rate of statutory sick pay specified in section 157(1) in pounds`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "must not be less than the weekly rate of statutory sick pay"

-- Plain gloss: where a daily rate is needed, it is one seventh of the weekly rate.
-- src:3308 | (4) Where for any purpose of this Part of this Act or of regulations it is necessary to calculate the daily rate of statutory maternity pay, the amount payable by way of statutory maternity pay for any day shall be taken as one seventh of the weekly rate.
@ref s 166(4) (src:3308)
GIVEN `the weekly rate of statutory maternity pay in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 166(4) the daily rate of statutory maternity pay in pounds` MEANS
    `the weekly rate of statutory maternity pay in pounds` DIVIDED BY 7

§§ `s 167 Funding of employers' liabilities`

-- The percentage of payments that do not qualify for small employers' relief that may be recovered.
-- src:3314 | (ii) an amount equal to 92 per cent of the aggregate of such of those payments as do not so qualify; and
@ref s 167(2)(a)(ii) (src:3314)
`s 167(2)(a)(ii) the percentage recoverable of payments that do not qualify for small employers' relief` MEANS 92%

-- Plain gloss: the regulations must let a payer recover all the payments that qualify for small
-- employers' relief plus 92 per cent of those that do not. "Except in prescribed circumstances"
-- means regulations may cut this back, so the amount is what the regulations must allow at most
-- before such circumstances.
-- src:3312 | (a) make provision for a person who has made a payment of statutory maternity pay to be entitled, except in prescribed circumstances, to recover an amount equal to the sum of—
-- src:3313 | (i) the aggregate of such of those payments as qualify for small employers’ relief; and
-- src:3314 | (ii) an amount equal to 92 per cent of the aggregate of such of those payments as do not so qualify; and
@ref s 167(2)(a) (src:3312-3314)
GIVEN `the aggregate of the payments of statutory maternity pay that qualify for small employers' relief, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the aggregate of the payments of statutory maternity pay that do not so qualify, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 167(2)(a) the amount the regulations must provide for the payer to be entitled to recover in pounds` MEANS
    `the aggregate of the payments of statutory maternity pay that qualify for small employers' relief, in pounds`
        PLUS (`the aggregate of the payments of statutory maternity pay that do not so qualify, in pounds`
                  TIMES `s 167(2)(a)(ii) the percentage recoverable of payments that do not qualify for small employers' relief`)

-- Plain gloss: a payment qualifies for small employers' relief when, in relation to that
-- woman's maternity pay period, the person who has to pay is a small employer. "Small employer" has
-- the meaning regulations give it (subsection (4)), so that fact is asked as an input.
-- src:3319 | (3) For the purposes of this section a payment of statutory maternity pay which a person is liable to make to a woman qualifies for small employers’ relief if, in relation to that woman’s maternity pay period, the person liable to make the payment is a small employer.
@ref s 167(3) (src:3319)
GIVEN `the person liable to make the payment is a small employer in relation to that woman's maternity pay period` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 167(3) the payment qualifies for small employers' relief` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "qualifies for small employers' relief if" (marginal reading (a): a failed "if" counts as the text's no)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "small employer" has the meaning assigned by regulations (subsection (4))
    `the person liable to make the payment is a small employer in relation to that woman's maternity pay period`

-- Plain gloss: an amount deducted from the employer's contributions payments under the funding
-- regulations is treated as paid, and as received by the Commissioners, towards the employer's
-- Class 1 liability, except in prescribed cases.
-- src:3329 | (6) Where in accordance with any provision of regulations under subsection (1) an amount has been deducted from an employer’s contributions payments, the amount so deducted shall (except in such cases as may be prescribed) be treated for the purposes of any provision made by or under any enactment in relation to primary or secondary Class 1 contributions—
-- src:3330 | (a) as having been paid (on such date as may be determined in accordance with the regulations), and
-- src:3331 | (b) as having been received by the Commissioners of Inland Revenue,
-- src:3332 | towards discharging the employer’s liability in respect of such contributions.
@ref s 167(6) (src:3329-3332)
GIVEN `an amount has been deducted from the employer's contributions payments in accordance with a provision of regulations under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the case is one prescribed under subsection (6)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 167(6) the amount deducted is treated as paid towards discharging the employer's liability` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the case is one prescribed under subsection (6)`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "except in such cases as may be prescribed"
        IF `an amount has been deducted from the employer's contributions payments in accordance with a provision of regulations under subsection (1)`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the treatment is of "the amount so deducted"

-- Plain gloss: "contributions payments" are payments the employer is required by an enactment
-- to make in discharge of a liability as employer for primary or secondary Class 1 contributions.
-- src:3334 | (8) In this section “contributions payments”, in relation to an employer, means any payments which the employer is required, by or under any enactment, to make in discharge of any liability in respect of primary or secondary Class 1 contributions.
@ref s 167(8) (src:3334)
GIVEN `the payment is required, by or under an enactment, to be made by the employer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the payment is made in discharge of a liability in respect of primary or secondary Class 1 contributions` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 167(8) the payment is a contributions payment` MEANS
    IF     `the payment is required, by or under an enactment, to be made by the employer`
       AND `the payment is made in discharge of a liability in respect of primary or secondary Class 1 contributions`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "means any payments which the employer is required, by or under any enactment, to make in discharge of any liability in respect of primary or secondary Class 1 contributions"

§§ `s 168 Relationship with benefits and other payments`

-- Plain gloss: Schedule 13 says how statutory maternity pay interacts with other benefits and
-- payments. The Schedule is in another part's range, so its answer is an input (seam found by
-- ssc6, not in the plan's register).
-- src:3335 | 168 Relationship with benefits and other payments etc.
-- src:3336 | Schedule 13 to this Act has effect with respect to the relationship between statutory maternity pay and certain benefits and payments.
@ref s 168 (src:3335-3336)
GIVEN `sch 13 the relationship between statutory maternity pay and the benefit or payment is as Schedule 13 provides` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 168 Schedule 13 has effect with respect to the relationship between statutory maternity pay and the benefit or payment` MEANS
    `sch 13 the relationship between statutory maternity pay and the benefit or payment is as Schedule 13 provides`        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text) when Schedule 13 is not consulted

§§ `s 169 Crown employment`

-- Plain gloss: a woman employed by or under the Crown is treated as any other woman employee.
-- (Unlike s 161, this section has no carve-out for the armed forces.)
-- src:3337 | 169 Crown employment - Part XII.
-- src:3338 | The provisions of this Part of this Act apply in relation to women employed by or under the Crown as they apply in relation to women employed otherwise than by or under the Crown.
@ref s 169 (src:3337-3338)
GIVEN `the woman is employed by or under the Crown` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 169 Part XII applies to the woman as it applies to women employed otherwise than by or under the Crown` MEANS
    IF `the woman is employed by or under the Crown`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the section speaks only of women employed by or under the Crown

§§ `s 170 Special classes of persons`

-- Plain gloss: regulations can change Part XII for a woman employed on a ship, vessel, hovercraft
-- or aircraft, or outside Great Britain at a prescribed time, or in prescribed employment on the
-- continental shelf. The power itself is machinery (the coverage table says so); what it means for
-- her is whether she is in a class it can reach. Seam S25 supplies the s 120(2) definition.
-- src:3340 | (1) The Secretary of State may with the concurrence of the Treasury make regulations modifying this Part of this Act in such manner as he thinks proper in their application to any person who is, has been or is to be—
-- src:3341 | (a) employed on board any ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft;
-- src:3342 | (b) outside Great Britain at any prescribed time or in any prescribed circumstances; or
-- src:3343 | (c) in prescribed employment in connection with continental shelf operations, as defined in section 120(2) above.
@ref s 170(1) (src:3340-3343)
GIVEN `she is employed on board a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft` IS A BOOLEAN
      `she is outside Great Britain at a prescribed time or in prescribed circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 120(2) the employment is in connection with continental shelf operations` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employment is prescribed employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 170(1) regulations may modify Part XII in its application to her` MEANS
    IF     `she is employed on board a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft`
        OR `she is outside Great Britain at a prescribed time or in prescribed circumstances`
        OR (`the employment is prescribed employment` AND `s 120(2) the employment is in connection with continental shelf operations`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the power reaches persons "employed on board any ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft", "outside Great Britain" or in "prescribed employment in connection with continental shelf operations"

§§ `s 171 Interpretation of Part XII and supplementary provisions`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- "confinement" (s 171(1)).  The core's `Labour` carries the weeks of pregnancy when labour began,
-- whether the labour resulted in the issue of a living child, and the day of the issue. The second
-- limb asks for "the issue of a child whether alive or dead", which `Labour` has no field for (it
-- has only "a living child"), so a plain input carries it (NEEDS-CORE.md).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Plain gloss: she is "confined" if her labour produces a living child, or if labour after 24
-- weeks of pregnancy produces a child alive or dead. A miscarriage before then is not a confinement.
-- src:3351 | “confinement” means—
-- src:3352 | labour resulting in the issue of a living child, or
-- src:3353 | labour after 24 weeks of pregnancy resulting in the issue of a child whether alive or dead,
-- src:3354 | and “confined” shall be construed accordingly; and where a woman’s labour begun on one day results in the issue of a child on another day she shall be taken to be confined on the day of the issue of the child or, if labour results in the issue of twins or a greater number of children, she shall be taken to be confined on the day of the issue of the last of them;
@ref s 171(1) "confinement" (src:3351-3354)
GIVEN labour IS A Labour
      `the labour resulted in the issue of a child, alive or dead` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171(1) she is confined` MEANS
    IF     labour's `labour resulted in the issue of a living child`
        OR (   labour's `weeks of pregnancy when labour began` GREATER THAN `the weeks of pregnancy named in the stillbirth rule`
           AND `the labour resulted in the issue of a child, alive or dead`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“confinement” means— labour resulting in the issue of a living child, or labour after 24 weeks of pregnancy resulting in the issue of a child whether alive or dead"

-- Plain gloss: the day she is confined is the day the child is issued, or the day the last of
-- twins or more is issued, even if the labour began the day before.
@ref s 171(1) "confinement" (src:3351-3354)
GIVEN labour IS A Labour
      `the labour resulted in the issue of a child, alive or dead` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 171(1) the day on which she is confined` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 171(1) she is confined` labour `the labour resulted in the issue of a child, alive or dead`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST (labour's `the day of the issue of the child, or of the last of the children`)
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): a woman who is not confined has no day of confinement
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent)

-- Plain gloss: the answer ssc9's Schedule 11 and s 153(11) ask for (seam S32). The pregnancy has
-- ended, and not by a confinement.
@ref s 171(1) "confinement" (src:3351-3354)
GIVEN `the pregnancy has terminated` IS A BOOLEAN
      labour IS A Labour
      `the labour resulted in the issue of a child, alive or dead` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171(1) the pregnancy terminated otherwise than by confinement` MEANS
    IF NOT `the pregnancy has terminated`
    THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "confinement" is defined for a labour; the text says nothing of a pregnancy still running
    ELSE CONSIDER `s 171(1) she is confined` labour `the labour resulted in the issue of a child, alive or dead`
         WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“confinement” means— labour resulting in the issue of a living child, or labour after 24 weeks of pregnancy resulting in the issue of a child whether alive or dead" (a termination that is a confinement is not one "otherwise than by confinement")
         WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- Plain gloss: "dismissed" is read as Part X of the Employment Rights Act 1996 reads it. That Act
-- is not among the sources, so whether she was dismissed in that sense is asked as a fact.
-- src:3355 | “dismissed” is to be construed in accordance with Part X of the Employment Rights Act 1996;
@ref s 171(1) "dismissed" (src:3355)
GIVEN `she was dismissed within the meaning of Part X of the Employment Rights Act 1996` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171(1) she was dismissed` MEANS
    `she was dismissed within the meaning of Part X of the Employment Rights Act 1996`        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "is to be construed in accordance with Part X of the Employment Rights Act 1996"

-- Plain gloss: an "employee" is a woman who is gainfully employed in Great Britain, under a
-- contract of service or in an office, with earnings (as Parts 1 to 5 use that word); but regulations
-- can exclude some such women and include some others. The converter shows "..." at the end of the
-- first limb and a line of dots after it (src:3357-3358): words and a limb repealed. Whether the
-- work done on the sea adjacent to Great Britain is "in Great Britain" is s 172(a), asked as an input.
-- src:3356 | “employee” means a woman who is—
-- src:3357 | gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in an office (including elective office) with earnings (within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5 above); ...
-- src:3359 | but subject to regulations made with the concurrence of Her Majesty’s Revenue and Customs which may provide for cases where any such woman is not to be treated as an employee for the purposes of this Part of this Act and for cases where a woman who would not otherwise be an employee for those purposes is to be treated as an employee for those purposes;
@ref s 171(1) "employee" (src:3356-3359)
GIVEN employment IS AN Employment
      `she is gainfully employed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the work is carried on in the territorial waters adjacent to Great Britain` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 172(a) a reference to Great Britain includes the place` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the employment is with earnings within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations made with the concurrence of Her Majesty's Revenue and Customs provide that she is not to be treated as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations so made provide that she is to be treated as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171(1) she is an employee` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `regulations made with the concurrence of Her Majesty's Revenue and Customs provide that she is not to be treated as an employee` AND `regulations so made provide that she is to be treated as an employee`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the two kinds of regulation cannot both apply to one woman, and the text does not say which prevails
        IF `regulations so made provide that she is to be treated as an employee` AND isWoman
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `regulations made with the concurrence of Her Majesty's Revenue and Customs provide that she is not to be treated as an employee`
            THEN JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "cases where any such woman is not to be treated as an employee for the purposes of this Part of this Act"
        OTHERWISE definition
    WHERE
        isWoman MEANS employment's `the employee`'s `sex` EQUALS Woman
        inGreatBritain MEANS
            IF employment's `carried on in Great Britain`
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE IF `the work is carried on in the territorial waters adjacent to Great Britain`
                 THEN `s 172(a) a reference to Great Britain includes the place`
                 ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a woman who is gainfully employed in Great Britain" (work outside Great Britain and outside the adjacent waters)
        restOfDefinition MEANS
                isWoman
            AND `she is gainfully employed`
            AND (employment's `held under a contract of service` OR employment's `held in an office`)
            AND `the employment is with earnings within the meaning of Parts 1 to 5`
        definition MEANS
            CONSIDER inGreatBritain
            WHEN NOTHING THEN
                IF restOfDefinition
                THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the adjacent waters are in Great Britain is s 172(a)'s answer
                ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a woman who is gainfully employed in Great Britain" (a limb other than the place already fails)
            WHEN JUST inside THEN
                IF inside AND restOfDefinition
                THEN JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employee” means a woman who is gainfully employed in Great Britain either under a contract of service or in an office (including elective office) with earnings"

-- Plain gloss: the "employer" of a woman is the person who, under s 6, has to pay secondary Class 1
-- contributions on her earnings, or who would have to but for the s 6(1)(b) condition or because
-- she is under 16. Whether s 6 makes someone liable is ssc1's answer, asked as an input.
-- src:3360 | “employer”, in relation to a woman who is an employee, means a person who—
-- src:3361 | under section 6 above is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of her earnings; or
-- src:3362 | would be liable to pay such contributions but for—
-- src:3363 | the condition in section 6(1)(b), or
-- src:3364 | the employee being under the age of 16;
@ref s 171(1) "employer" (src:3360-3364)
GIVEN `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of her earnings` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the condition in section 6(1)(b)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 173(a) the person is under 16` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171(1) the person is the employer in relation to her` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of her earnings` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the condition in section 6(1)(b)`
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` AND `s 173(a) the person is under 16` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `s 6 the person is liable to pay secondary Class 1 contributions in relation to any of her earnings` EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether s 6 makes the person liable is ssc1's answer
        IF `the person would be liable to pay such contributions but for the employee being under the age of 16` AND `s 173(a) the person is under 16` EQUALS NOTHING
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether she is under 16 is s 173(a)'s answer
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“employer”, in relation to a woman who is an employee, means a person who"

-- Plain gloss: "maternity pay period" is the period s 165(1) describes.
-- src:3365 | “maternity pay period” has the meaning assigned to it by section 165(1) above;
@ref s 171(1) "maternity pay period" (src:3365)
GIVEN `the first day of the maternity pay period` IS A DATE
      `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 171(1) the maternity pay period` MEANS
    `s 165(1) the maternity pay period` `the first day of the maternity pay period` `the duration of the prescribed period in weeks`

-- Plain gloss: "modifications" includes additions, omissions and amendments. It says "includes",
-- so another kind of change is not ruled out and is NOTHING.
DECLARE `s 171(1) Kind of change` IS ONE OF
    `s 171(1) an addition`
    `s 171(1) an omission`
    `s 171(1) an amendment`
    `s 171(1) another kind of change`

-- src:3366 | “modifications” includes additions, omissions and amendments, and related expressions shall be construed accordingly;
@ref s 171(1) "modifications" (src:3366)
GIVEN change IS A `s 171(1) Kind of change`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171(1) the change is a modification` MEANS
    CONSIDER change
    WHEN `s 171(1) an addition` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171(1) an omission` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171(1) an amendment` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 171(1) another kind of change` THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "“modifications” includes additions, omissions and amendments"

-- Plain gloss: in Part XII, "prescribed" means specified in, or determined in accordance with, regulations.
-- src:3367 | “prescribed” means specified in or determined in accordance with regulations;
@ref s 171(1) "prescribed" (src:3367)
GIVEN `it is specified in or determined in accordance with regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 171(1) the thing is prescribed` MEANS
    IF `it is specified in or determined in accordance with regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“prescribed” means specified in or determined in accordance with regulations"

-- Plain gloss: her normal weekly earnings are the average weekly earnings paid to her, or for her
-- benefit, under the contract of service with the employer in question during the "relevant period"
-- (subsections (4) and (5)). "Earnings" and "relevant period" have the meanings regulations give
-- them (so the caller supplies the earnings and the number of weeks), and in prescribed cases
-- regulations calculate them instead (subsection (6)).
-- src:3381 | (4) For the purposes of this Part of this Act a woman’s normal weekly earnings shall, subject to subsection (6) below, be taken to be the average weekly earnings which in the relevant period have been paid to her or paid for her benefit under the contract of service with the employer in question.
-- src:3382 | (5) For the purposes of subsection (4) above “earnings” and “relevant period” shall have the meanings given to them by regulations.
-- src:3383 | (6) In such cases as may be prescribed a woman’s normal weekly earnings shall be calculated in accordance with regulations.
@ref s 171(4) (src:3381-3383)
GIVEN `the earnings paid to her or for her benefit under the contract of service with the employer in question in the relevant period, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of weeks in the relevant period` IS A NUMBER
      `the case is one in which regulations require her normal weekly earnings to be calculated in accordance with regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 171(4) her normal weekly earnings in pounds` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the case is one in which regulations require her normal weekly earnings to be calculated in accordance with regulations`
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "In such cases as may be prescribed a woman's normal weekly earnings shall be calculated in accordance with regulations"
        IF `the number of weeks in the relevant period` AT MOST 0
            THEN NOTHING        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "relevant period" has the meaning regulations give it; a period of no weeks has no average
        OTHERWISE JUST (`the earnings paid to her or for her benefit under the contract of service with the employer in question in the relevant period, in pounds` DIVIDED BY `the number of weeks in the relevant period`)
